  </div>
  <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white/90 mb-3 sm:mb-4">Abstract</h2>

  <p class="text-[15px] sm:text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/90 font-light text-justify" data-reveal>
    Shot matching cut is pivotal for cinematic inter-shot coherence. Current multi-shot video generation methods primarily focus on superficial visual consistency, but often neglecting the fundamental <strong class="font-semibold text-white">Action Continuity</strong> essential for professional filmmaking, a technique known as <strong class="font-semibold text-white">Match on Action (Action-Cut)</strong>. Consequently, state-of-the-art models frequently exhibit severe artifacts during transitions, including action resetting, temporal flickering, and physical logic disruptions.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[15px] sm:text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/90 font-light text-justify" data-reveal style="transition-delay:80ms">
    To address these limitations, we propose <strong class="font-semibold text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-[#ff4d4d] to-[#ffb84d]">Act2Cut</strong>, the first unified framework specifically designed for continuous-action-driven multi-shot video generation. Architecturally, we introduce <strong class="font-semibold text-white">Global-Local-Shot Rotary Position Embedding (GLoS-RoPE)</strong>, a hierarchical temporal RoPE that preserves global narrative flow while managing local shot evolution. To anchor physical integrity, we propose <strong class="font-semibold text-white">Transitional Boundary Residual (TBR)</strong> to enforce action state consistency between adjacent shots' tail and head frames. Furthermore, <strong class="font-semibold text-white">Shot Causal Mask (SCM)</strong> and <strong class="font-semibold text-white">Hierarchical Context Mask (HCM)</strong> are integrated to model inter-shot causality and eliminate semantic leakage across distinct cinematic descriptions.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[15px] sm:text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/90 font-light text-justify" data-reveal style="transition-delay:160ms">
    For training, we construct <strong class="font-semibold text-white">ActCutVid-200K</strong>, a large-scale, hierarchically annotated dataset featuring decoupled cinematographic languages and <strong class="font-semibold text-white">Focus-of-Attention (FoA)</strong> guidance, sourced from films, TV series, and AI-generated content. Based on this, we establish <strong class="font-semibold text-white">ActCutBench</strong>, a specialized benchmark for the Action-Cut task, evaluating narrative consistency, visual coherence, action continuity, and cinematographic language alignment. To unify diverse generation paradigms within one model, we employ multi-task tuning to enable native one-pass generation and prediction for Text-to-Video, Image-to-Video, Storyboard-to-Video, and Video-to-Video. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Act2Cut effectively maintains consistency and coherence while enabling action continuity.
  </p>
</section>
